  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-2)=3$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=3$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
